00:04:01.160 --> 00:04:03.010
capabilities.

00:04:03.010 --> 00:04:05.790
So let's take a brief
moment to look at some

00:04:05.790 --> 00:04:09.512
of the key features of GCS.

00:04:09.512 --> 00:04:11.220
So I talked about the
unified experience.

00:04:11.220 --> 00:04:15.740
Why unified experience is great
is whether you are using GCS

00:04:15.740 --> 00:04:18.180
for analytics or
machine learning,

00:04:18.180 --> 00:04:21.420
or you are using GCS
for backup or archival,

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:23.520
across the whole
range of different use

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:29.120
cases with the unified APIs, you
do not have to redo your code,

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:31.650
or you don't have to
re-architect your systems

00:04:31.650 --> 00:04:36.690
because GCS has the same API
across all storage classes.

00:04:36.690 --> 00:04:39.660
And it has the same latencies.

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:43.650
Another great feature that GCS
has is this strong consistency.

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:45.690
GCS is a strongly
consistent system.

00:04:45.690 --> 00:04:47.400
We are not eventually
consistent.

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:48.060
Right.

00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:49.770
So what that means
is, if you are

00:04:49.770 --> 00:04:53.370
using GCS to store
your data in a region

00:04:53.370 --> 00:04:57.430
and you are writing to
certain files in one region,

00:04:57.430 --> 00:05:00.480
now, in a very different
part of the geography--

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:03.120
say in the exact opposite
place on the globe--

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:05.940
if you try to list
GCS, you are going

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:09.640
to find the entire listing
is strongly consistent.

00:05:09.640 --> 00:05:12.240
So this has tremendous
benefits in that, say,

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:14.790
if we didn't have strong
consistency and, actually, GCS

00:05:14.790 --> 00:05:16.680
is the only planet
scale storage system

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:18.520
with such strong consistency.

00:05:18.520 --> 00:05:21.000
So if you are not
using GCS, most likely

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:23.820
to solve an analytics pipeline
use case like this, what you

00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:26.160
would do is you would have
an object storage, which

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:27.240
is your data lake.

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:29.950
But you'd also use a
key value database,

00:05:29.950 --> 00:05:32.460
which you are using for
your metadata consistency.

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:35.220
But, if you use GCS, you don't
need to use any other systems.

00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:36.930
GCS takes care of it.

00:05:36.930 --> 00:05:39.780
GCS is also greatly
redundant, due redundant.

00:05:39.780 --> 00:05:41.340
Depending on what
is your use case,

00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:44.100
you can use storage
classes which gives you

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:48.660
regional redundancy, or a
redundancy within a country,

00:05:48.660 --> 00:05:51.400
or you can even choose with
multi regional redundancy,

00:05:51.400 --> 00:05:54.030
which is across an
entire continent.

00:05:54.030 --> 00:05:56.820
And lastly, GCSE is
scalable to exabytes.

00:05:56.820 --> 00:05:59.280
Like you don't have to worry
about scalability at all

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:01.170
if you are dealing with GCS.

00:06:01.170 --> 00:06:03.990
So sometimes new customers
coming from on prem,

00:06:03.990 --> 00:06:07.050
they ask me I'm going
to bring hundreds

00:06:07.050 --> 00:06:08.280
of terabytes or petabytes.

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:09.330
Is it going to scale?

00:06:09.330 --> 00:06:09.897
Right.

00:06:09.897 --> 00:06:11.730
So you don't have any
such worries with GCS.

00:06:11.730 --> 00:06:13.770
In fact, in GCS we
have single customers

00:06:13.770 --> 00:06:17.250
who have more than
exabytes of data.

00:06:17.250 --> 00:06:19.473
So, depending on what
is your use case,

00:06:19.473 --> 00:06:21.390
you are going to use
different storage classes

00:06:21.390 --> 00:06:23.223
or you're going to use
the different storage

00:06:23.223 --> 00:06:25.080
classes in conjunction.

00:06:25.080 --> 00:06:28.350
Now if your use case is
content distribution,

00:06:28.350 --> 00:06:30.870
if you are streaming
video, or multimedia,

00:06:30.870 --> 00:06:33.540
or you are hosting
website information,

00:06:33.540 --> 00:06:36.210
or if you have a business
continuity disaster

00:06:36.210 --> 00:06:38.700
recovery use case,
multi-regional

00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:40.040
is great for you.

00:06:40.040 --> 00:06:41.790
One thing to remember
about multi regional

00:06:41.790 --> 00:06:45.510
is multi regional storage
class has inbuilt smart

00:06:45.510 --> 00:06:50.620
caching and smart routing
inbuilt in the storage class

00:06:50.620 --> 00:06:51.120
itself.

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:55.590
So you don't have to
necessarily use a CDN at all.

00:06:55.590 --> 00:06:58.890
If, on the other hand, your
workload is very compute

00:06:58.890 --> 00:07:00.660
intensive, so what
you are trying to do

00:07:00.660 --> 00:07:02.640
is basically do a lot
of number crunching,

00:07:02.640 --> 00:07:04.680
you may be running
an analytics job,

00:07:04.680 --> 00:07:07.470
or you may be doing genomic
processing, or maybe just

00:07:07.470 --> 00:07:08.310
general compute--

00:07:08.310 --> 00:07:10.050
if that's what you
want to do, what you

00:07:10.050 --> 00:07:13.590
are looking for is your
storage and your compute

00:07:13.590 --> 00:07:15.390
to be co-located.

00:07:15.390 --> 00:07:17.550
And that is where
regional storage classes

00:07:17.550 --> 00:07:20.400
are really useful,
because you predictably

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:22.470
know this is exactly
where my storage is.

00:07:22.470 --> 00:07:26.520
You can spin up your compute
instances right there.

00:07:26.520 --> 00:07:29.760
Now recently we also
launched a storage class

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:32.400
in beta, which is
called dual regional.

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:34.590
And dual regional
really marries the best

00:07:34.590 --> 00:07:36.340
of multi-regional and regional.

00:07:36.340 --> 00:07:38.707
So dual regional-- what
dual regional is it

00:07:38.707 --> 00:07:41.940
is a pair of two
specific regions.

00:07:41.940 --> 00:07:43.470
So you exactly know
where your data

00:07:43.470 --> 00:07:45.900
is so you can
co-locate your compute.

00:07:45.900 --> 00:07:49.740
So it's great for analytics or
ML or other computer use cases.

00:07:49.740 --> 00:07:51.900
But it also has the
benefits of multi-regional

00:07:51.900 --> 00:07:54.640
in that it is geodistributed
across two different regions

00:07:54.640 --> 00:07:57.490
so it gives you the protection
for disaster recovery.

00:07:57.490 --> 00:08:00.510
Near line and goal line are
great if what you are looking

00:08:00.510 --> 00:08:02.070
for is backup and archive.

00:08:02.070 --> 00:08:04.200
And for those of
you who are really

00:08:04.200 --> 00:08:07.210
on top of your game and you have
read our blog post this morning

00:08:07.210 --> 00:08:09.720
"What is cooler than cold?"

00:08:09.720 --> 00:08:13.110
we also announced today an
archive storage class which

00:08:13.110 --> 00:08:15.120
is even colder than cold line.

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:18.000
But the beauty of our
cold storage architecture

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:21.180
is at the same time we
support even that storage

00:08:21.180 --> 00:08:23.995
class with the same API and
the same consistent subsecond

00:08:23.995 --> 00:08:24.495
latency.

00:08:27.240 --> 00:08:29.400
So, across all these
different use cases,

00:08:29.400 --> 00:08:31.770
we have a lot of
customers who use

00:08:31.770 --> 00:08:34.020
GCS in a very versatile manner.

00:08:36.809 --> 00:08:39.000
I touched up on this
point initially when

00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:43.650
I started talking about GCS,
but it is worth keeping in mind.

00:08:43.650 --> 00:08:45.900
The way most people
use GCS, they

00:08:45.900 --> 00:08:50.670
don't think of GCS as a
point storage product.

00:08:50.670 --> 00:08:54.270
From the earlier days of
on prem enterprise storage,

00:08:54.270 --> 00:08:56.700
you might be conditioned
to thinking about storage

00:08:56.700 --> 00:08:58.770
in a very particular way.

00:08:58.770 --> 00:09:00.790
But GCS is very versatile.

00:09:00.790 --> 00:09:05.010
We see very often GCS
being used as a data lake.

00:09:05.010 --> 00:09:07.860
GCS is the first place
your data resides

00:09:07.860 --> 00:09:10.590
when you think of doing
analytics, or machine learning,

00:09:10.590 --> 00:09:13.150
or whatever really
you want to do.

00:09:13.150 --> 00:09:15.780
So GCS is your entry
to the entire data

00:09:15.780 --> 00:09:18.260
platform that is Google.

00:09:18.260 --> 00:09:21.180
Now let's start
talking about security.

00:09:21.180 --> 00:09:25.260
So why security by
design and default?

00:09:25.260 --> 00:09:28.620
So, at Google, we don't
think about security

00:09:28.620 --> 00:09:29.940
as an afterthought.

00:09:29.940 --> 00:09:31.440
We don't think
security is something

00:09:31.440 --> 00:09:33.100
which can be bolted on later.

00:09:33.100 --> 00:09:36.300
So, even as we
design our systems

00:09:36.300 --> 00:09:38.670
and design our products
from the ground up,

00:09:38.670 --> 00:09:41.020
we design security side by side.

00:09:41.020 --> 00:09:44.830
So this has reflection in
various different dimensions.

00:09:44.830 --> 00:09:47.940
So Google has the
largest private network

00:09:47.940 --> 00:09:51.210
of any public cloud
provider out there.

00:09:51.210 --> 00:09:53.970
We literally have thousands
of edge node locations

00:09:53.970 --> 00:09:57.610
and hundreds of pop node
locations across the globe.

00:09:57.610 --> 00:09:59.910
So chances are, wherever
you are, wherever

00:09:59.910 --> 00:10:03.060
you are writing from,
we have an edge or a pop

00:10:03.060 --> 00:10:04.690
right next to you.

00:10:04.690 --> 00:10:07.740
So what happens is
your data packets

00:10:07.740 --> 00:10:12.870
enter these GCS' or Google's
private network at the closest

00:10:12.870 --> 00:10:16.620
point of entry and leaves
Google's private network

00:10:16.620 --> 00:10:18.990
at the latest point of exit.

00:10:18.990 --> 00:10:20.910
So this has tremendous
performance benefits

00:10:20.910 --> 00:10:22.640
because it's really fast.

00:10:22.640 --> 00:10:24.000
It's a private network.

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:26.880
It has tremendous
cost benefits for you,

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:30.000
because you are not paying
much for network egress

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:31.770
outside of our networks.

00:10:31.770 --> 00:10:34.890
But, most importantly, it has
tremendous security benefits

00:10:34.890 --> 00:10:36.010
as well.

00:10:36.010 --> 00:10:38.940
So for those of
you who are really

00:10:38.940 --> 00:10:41.770
experienced with security, you
know security and performance

00:10:41.770 --> 00:10:43.470
do not always go together.

00:10:43.470 --> 00:10:45.900
This is one place where it does.

00:10:45.900 --> 00:10:49.710
So the fact that we have such
fabulous private networking,

00:10:49.710 --> 00:10:51.310
you can get benefits
of performance

00:10:51.310 --> 00:10:53.490
and you can get
benefits of security.

00:10:53.490 --> 00:10:55.500
So consider this example.

00:10:55.500 --> 00:10:57.190
Say you are trying
to write your data

00:10:57.190 --> 00:11:00.540
or read your data from
your region to two

00:11:00.540 --> 00:11:03.990
different regions,
region A and region B.

00:11:03.990 --> 00:11:07.230
So with any other public cloud
network, what would happen

00:11:07.230 --> 00:11:09.990
is your data packets
are going to flow

00:11:09.990 --> 00:11:15.930
through the public internet for
most of the distance and then

00:11:15.930 --> 00:11:19.170
just when you hit your
specific region that's

00:11:19.170 --> 00:11:23.040
when your data packages are to
go through your cloud vendor's

00:11:23.040 --> 00:11:23.940
network.

00:11:23.940 --> 00:11:26.670
With Google, on the other hand,
what happens is regardless

00:11:26.670 --> 00:11:28.980
of where your data is stored--
your data will be stored

00:11:28.980 --> 00:11:30.540
in a very faraway region--

00:11:30.540 --> 00:11:33.580
but you are within Google's
pop at the nearest point

00:11:33.580 --> 00:11:34.810
which is close to you.

00:11:34.810 --> 00:11:39.340
So this gives you absolutely
minimum exposure of your data

00:11:39.340 --> 00:11:43.920
packets on the public internet.

00:11:43.920 --> 00:11:48.270
So, when we talk about
security or privacy,

00:11:48.270 --> 00:11:52.230
one of the most important
aspects of that is encryption.

00:11:52.230 --> 00:11:55.230
When you think about your
data in cloud, for example,

00:11:55.230 --> 00:11:58.920
you think of data at
rest and data in flight.

00:11:58.920 --> 00:12:01.590
Now, with GCS, you
do not have to worry.

00:12:01.590 --> 00:12:04.140
Whether it is your data
at rest or data in flight,

00:12:04.140 --> 00:12:06.840
your data is always encrypted.

00:12:06.840 --> 00:12:11.790
We do not believe in this
notion of default encryption

00:12:11.790 --> 00:12:13.080
as an option in the UI.

00:12:13.080 --> 00:12:18.510
We feel default and option
are sort of oxymoronic.

00:12:18.510 --> 00:12:21.510
So, all your data, whether
in flight or in motion,

00:12:21.510 --> 00:12:23.340
is always encrypted.

00:12:23.340 --> 00:12:26.160
We also have multiple layers
of encryption in our stack.

00:12:26.160 --> 00:12:31.680
So that goes with our
concept of defense in depth.

00:12:31.680 --> 00:12:33.900
Now, one caveat I would
like to mention here

00:12:33.900 --> 00:12:36.390
is for multi-cloud
interoperability,

00:12:36.390 --> 00:12:38.970
especially S3
compatibility, because S3

00:12:38.970 --> 00:12:40.980
supports HTTPN points.

00:12:40.980 --> 00:12:46.170
For S3 compatible API, we
also support unencrypted modes

00:12:46.170 --> 00:12:48.010
of data transfer.

00:12:48.010 --> 00:12:50.160
But, if you're
using GCS natively,

00:12:50.160 --> 00:12:52.480
all your data is
always encrypted.

00:12:52.480 --> 00:12:54.300
Now, what about
encryption key management?

00:12:54.300 --> 00:12:56.610
We have a range of
choices for you.

00:12:56.610 --> 00:13:00.210
If you want to manage and
host the encryption keys

00:13:00.210 --> 00:13:02.460
by yourself, you could do that.

00:13:02.460 --> 00:13:04.710
In which case, we wouldn't
know what is the encryption

00:13:04.710 --> 00:13:05.478
key you are using.

00:13:05.478 --> 00:13:07.770
In that case, you would just
specify the encryption key

00:13:07.770 --> 00:13:08.270
to us.

00:13:08.270 --> 00:13:11.730
It is called customer
specified encryption key.

00:13:11.730 --> 00:13:14.580
If you want us to host the
encryption key management

00:13:14.580 --> 00:13:17.100
service for you but you
want complete control

00:13:17.100 --> 00:13:19.710
in managing the
encryption key, we

00:13:19.710 --> 00:13:23.160
support customer managed
encryption key for you.

00:13:23.160 --> 00:13:24.990
There is also this
other range of use case

00:13:24.990 --> 00:13:28.950
where you want us to
help you with end to end

00:13:28.950 --> 00:13:30.720
encryption key management.

00:13:30.720 --> 00:13:33.880
For that, we have Google
managed encryption,

00:13:33.880 --> 00:13:36.190
which is everything is
automagical for you.

00:13:36.190 --> 00:13:37.750
We completely take
care of the fact

00:13:37.750 --> 00:13:40.870
that we have sophisticated
cutting edge encryption key

00:13:40.870 --> 00:13:43.030
management policies implemented.

00:13:43.030 --> 00:13:46.582
Now, auditing is another
important aspect in security,

00:13:46.582 --> 00:13:48.040
because you know
this is how you do

00:13:48.040 --> 00:13:49.960
your regulatory and compliance.

00:13:49.960 --> 00:13:52.510
But auditing is
also fundamentally

00:13:52.510 --> 00:13:55.540
how you prove to yourself
and how you can keep track

00:13:55.540 --> 00:13:57.460
that your security
posture is really working

00:13:57.460 --> 00:13:59.342
the way you intended it to be.

00:13:59.342 --> 00:14:01.300
And, again, depending on
what is your use case,

00:14:01.300 --> 00:14:03.280
we have various choices for you.

00:14:03.280 --> 00:14:06.070
If you use multiple
Google Cloud products

00:14:06.070 --> 00:14:10.510
and you want to do auditing
in a very uniform way,

00:14:10.510 --> 00:14:13.600
you can use our platform
level cloud audit logging.

00:14:13.600 --> 00:14:17.110
If you are, on the other hand,
a very savvy and very heavy

00:14:17.110 --> 00:14:19.560
GCS user and you
want to find out

00:14:19.560 --> 00:14:21.970
Nth level of detail
about how you are using

00:14:21.970 --> 00:14:24.110
the service like what's
your latencies, what's

00:14:24.110 --> 00:14:26.230
the throughput, lots
of other information,

00:14:26.230 --> 00:14:31.850
you can use GCS' custom
access and storage logs.

00:14:31.850 --> 00:14:36.890
Now, you may be a company
who have your own threat

00:14:36.890 --> 00:14:39.010
intelligence and log
monitoring system.

00:14:39.010 --> 00:14:41.510
And what you are looking for
is basically a programmatic way

00:14:41.510 --> 00:14:44.000
to consume all that audit
logs and channel them

00:14:44.000 --> 00:14:45.140
into your system.

00:14:45.140 --> 00:14:47.840
You can use stackdriver
monitoring to programmatically

00:14:47.840 --> 00:14:50.250
consume all our audit logs.

00:14:50.250 --> 00:14:54.290
Now, with all these
audit logs, you probably

00:14:54.290 --> 00:14:59.090
want to ensure that you comply
with certain third party audits

00:14:59.090 --> 00:15:00.420
and regulations.

00:15:00.420 --> 00:15:04.130
So we at Google definitely
help you with that.

00:15:04.130 --> 00:15:08.450
We can help support you with
a bunch of third party audits

00:15:08.450 --> 00:15:10.260
and regulations.

00:15:10.260 --> 00:15:13.370
And this is a continual pattern
of investment from our side

00:15:13.370 --> 00:15:14.580
that you would see.

00:15:14.580 --> 00:15:17.630
For example, in the last year
or so we launched bucket log.

00:15:17.630 --> 00:15:20.330
And, with that, when
configured correctly,

00:15:20.330 --> 00:15:26.700
we can help you with FINRA,
SEC, CFTC, and CFR regulations.

00:15:26.700 --> 00:15:30.950
Now another aspect of designing
systems where security

00:15:30.950 --> 00:15:35.720
is by design and
default is DLP is really

00:15:35.720 --> 00:15:37.190
a prime example of that.

00:15:37.190 --> 00:15:39.050
So DLP is data loss prevention.

00:15:39.050 --> 00:15:41.300
So I'll tell you how
our customers commonly

00:15:41.300 --> 00:15:44.250
use this special feature,
which is out there.

00:15:44.250 --> 00:15:46.580
So say you are a Financial
Services Institute

00:15:46.580 --> 00:15:53.540
and you have a data set which
you want to move to GCS.

00:15:53.540 --> 00:15:56.480
You want to use
GCS as a data lake.

00:15:56.480 --> 00:15:59.420
So you want to run your
analytics pipeline on top.

00:15:59.420 --> 00:16:01.460
But, before you run
your analytics pipeline,

00:16:01.460 --> 00:16:03.537
you want to ensure
that all the PII

00:16:03.537 --> 00:16:05.120
that you have in
your data-- you might

00:16:05.120 --> 00:16:07.610
have first name, last
names, email addresses.

00:16:07.610 --> 00:16:11.220
You might have social security
numbers, credit card numbers.

00:16:11.220 --> 00:16:14.270
So you want to ensure before you
run all the analytics pipeline

00:16:14.270 --> 00:16:16.490
all the data is redacted, right?

00:16:16.490 --> 00:16:17.990
It is desensitized.

00:16:17.990 --> 00:16:21.170
So for those of you who
are at an English session

00:16:21.170 --> 00:16:23.260
by our friends from
Scotiabank, you

00:16:23.260 --> 00:16:27.650
would see that right now DLP
has the single pane of glass,

00:16:27.650 --> 00:16:32.060
like literally one page
few clicks ability where

00:16:32.060 --> 00:16:36.170
you can specify an end to end
analytics pipeline with GCS.

00:16:36.170 --> 00:16:39.680
And you can completely
eliminate all the PII

00:16:39.680 --> 00:16:41.870
in your pipeline using DLP.

00:16:41.870 --> 00:16:44.990
Now DLP can help you
definitely with data reduction

00:16:44.990 --> 00:16:46.640
and desensitization.

00:16:46.640 --> 00:16:50.020
But even if the fundamental
thing that you want

00:16:50.020 --> 00:16:51.770
is to get a very good
understanding of how

00:16:51.770 --> 00:16:54.810
is your PII to visualize
and manage your PII,

00:16:54.810 --> 00:16:57.860
you can use DLP for that too.

00:16:57.860 --> 00:17:02.420
So these are all great examples
of security built in for you

00:17:02.420 --> 00:17:05.569
by design and default.
But, at the same time,

00:17:05.569 --> 00:17:07.849
from a customer use case
perspective and requirement

00:17:07.849 --> 00:17:12.480
perspective, also you need us
to build certain capabilities.

00:17:12.480 --> 00:17:18.470
So I want to briefly touch up on
a few customer-focused security

00:17:18.470 --> 00:17:22.130
capabilities that we
launched recently.

00:17:22.130 --> 00:17:26.810
In last year's Next, we
announced Cloud KMS in beta.

00:17:26.810 --> 00:17:29.690
Currently Cloud KMS is in GA.

00:17:29.690 --> 00:17:31.610
Cloud KMS allows you
to have complete end

00:17:31.610 --> 00:17:35.390
to end control for your
encryption key management.

00:17:35.390 --> 00:17:39.290
In Chicago Cloud Summit,
we launched bucket lock.

00:17:39.290 --> 00:17:41.330
And what bucket
lock basically is it

00:17:41.330 --> 00:17:42.780
is basically warm storage.

00:17:42.780 --> 00:17:43.280
Right.

00:17:43.280 --> 00:17:44.730
So it is immutable storage.

00:17:44.730 --> 00:17:47.690
So if your use case is I
want to write some data

00:17:47.690 --> 00:17:51.650
and specify a retention period
during which this data should

00:17:51.650 --> 00:17:54.245
be unmodified--

00:17:54.245 --> 00:17:55.620
I shouldn't be
able to modify it.

00:17:55.620 --> 00:17:57.510
I shouldn't be able
to delete this data--

00:17:57.510 --> 00:18:00.505
then bucket lock is the
right product for you.

00:18:00.505 --> 00:18:01.900
VPC service controls.

00:18:01.900 --> 00:18:05.610
So if your main concern is
data exfiltration, right?

00:18:05.610 --> 00:18:08.040
So say if you are
coming from on-prem,

00:18:08.040 --> 00:18:11.850
or you have your own private
data centers, so private cloud,

00:18:11.850 --> 00:18:14.730
and you want to ensure you
carry the same security

00:18:14.730 --> 00:18:18.630
posture as you
come to Google, VPC

00:18:18.630 --> 00:18:20.610
is the right product for you.

00:18:20.610 --> 00:18:24.300
And this is a
testament to the fact

00:18:24.300 --> 00:18:28.110
that we innovated when our
customers had requirements.

00:18:28.110 --> 00:18:31.770
In a very short span of time
we have over 5,000 projects

00:18:31.770 --> 00:18:32.670
using these features.

00:18:32.670 --> 00:18:34.650
We have over 5
billion operations.

00:18:34.650 --> 00:18:37.410
We have tens of petabytes
of data, which is already

00:18:37.410 --> 00:18:38.760
using these features.

00:18:38.760 --> 00:18:40.920
And we have over 100
petabytes of data

00:18:40.920 --> 00:18:44.130
which is planned for migration
using these features, right?

00:18:44.130 --> 00:18:47.460
So this is a continual
pattern that you would see,

00:18:47.460 --> 00:18:51.810
where we at Google want to
ensure that we innovate exactly

00:18:51.810 --> 00:18:53.730
where you need us
to innovate, where

00:18:53.730 --> 00:18:55.530
you have your requirements.

00:18:55.530 --> 00:18:58.110
And to continue in
this vein, I would now

00:18:58.110 --> 00:19:00.660
like to invite my
colleague Risham, who's

00:19:00.660 --> 00:19:03.100
got a bunch of exciting
new announcements for you.

00:19:03.100 --> 00:19:04.528
Enjoy.

00:19:04.528 --> 00:19:06.464
[APPLAUSE]

00:19:09.298 --> 00:19:10.840
RISHAM DHILLON:
Thank you, Subhasish.

00:19:10.840 --> 00:19:11.780
Hello, everyone.

00:19:11.780 --> 00:19:12.550
Welcome.

00:19:12.550 --> 00:19:15.760
Today we're going to announce
four new features related

00:19:15.760 --> 00:19:17.540
to GCS security.

00:19:17.540 --> 00:19:19.005
So let's get started.

00:19:19.005 --> 00:19:20.630
The first feature
that we're announcing

00:19:20.630 --> 00:19:23.920
is VPC Service Controls,
which is now in GA.

00:19:23.920 --> 00:19:25.750
With VPC Service
Controls you can

00:19:25.750 --> 00:19:29.050
make your own virtual
private cloud within GCP.

00:19:29.050 --> 00:19:31.540
So customers that come from
traditional enterprise,

00:19:31.540 --> 00:19:34.540
on premise environments, may
find it challenging sometimes

00:19:34.540 --> 00:19:37.900
to re-architect their network
perimeters and public clouds.

00:19:37.900 --> 00:19:40.780
And this is exactly the problem
that VPC Service Controls

00:19:40.780 --> 00:19:42.830
is going to help you solve.

00:19:42.830 --> 00:19:44.620
So, with VPC Service
Controls, you

00:19:44.620 --> 00:19:48.100
can very easily set up a network
perimeter or network firewall,

00:19:48.100 --> 00:19:51.190
keep certain resources within
that perimeter-- for example,

00:19:51.190 --> 00:19:54.010
your GCS instance, or
your BigQuery instance--

00:19:54.010 --> 00:19:56.620
allow those resources to be
able to talk with each other,

00:19:56.620 --> 00:20:00.020
and block access to resources
outside of the perimeter.

00:20:00.020 --> 00:20:03.310
So, in this image here, you can
see that the green represents

00:20:03.310 --> 00:20:06.070
the virtual private cloud,
whereas the red represents

00:20:06.070 --> 00:20:08.440
services that don't have access.

00:20:08.440 --> 00:20:10.240
Now, with VPC
Service Controls, you

00:20:10.240 --> 00:20:14.210
can also configure and extend
the firewall as you prefer.

00:20:14.210 --> 00:20:17.890
You can wait less certain
IP addresses, as well.

00:20:17.890 --> 00:20:20.800
VPC Service Controls really
gives you a strong foundation

00:20:20.800 --> 00:20:22.750
to build your own
virtual private cloud,

00:20:22.750 --> 00:20:26.000
and we hope you get
the feature a try.

00:20:26.000 --> 00:20:28.460
Next we'd like to announce
that bucket policy only

00:20:28.460 --> 00:20:30.080
is now in beta.

00:20:30.080 --> 00:20:32.300
Bucket policy only
makes the process

00:20:32.300 --> 00:20:35.030
of managing permissions
for enterprise companies

00:20:35.030 --> 00:20:36.660
a lot easier.

00:20:36.660 --> 00:20:39.380
So, with bucket policy
only, all you have to do

00:20:39.380 --> 00:20:43.520
is set bucket level IAM policy,
and then any object or data

00:20:43.520 --> 00:20:47.000
that you upload to your bucket
will adhere to the policy

00:20:47.000 --> 00:20:49.200
that you set at
the bucket level.

00:20:49.200 --> 00:20:50.990
So, if you take a
step back for a moment

00:20:50.990 --> 00:20:54.290
and imagine what it's like
to have thousands of buckets

00:20:54.290 --> 00:20:55.850
with millions of
objects in them,

00:20:55.850 --> 00:20:57.890
or even hundreds of
millions of objects,

00:20:57.890 --> 00:21:00.620
with millions of
object ACLs in them,

00:21:00.620 --> 00:21:03.920
it can become quite apparent
that managing object

00:21:03.920 --> 00:21:08.270
level ACLs is sometimes hard
and difficult. And with bucket

00:21:08.270 --> 00:21:10.230
policy only you no
longer have to do that.

00:21:10.230 --> 00:21:12.650
You can just set a
bucket level IAM policy,

00:21:12.650 --> 00:21:15.320
and rest assured knowing that
everything in your bucket

00:21:15.320 --> 00:21:17.670
adheres to those permissions.

00:21:17.670 --> 00:21:20.150
So with bucket policy only
you get this added benefit

00:21:20.150 --> 00:21:24.230
of preventing data exposure--
accidental data exposure.

00:21:24.230 --> 00:21:27.320
And the reason why that is is
because now you no longer have

00:21:27.320 --> 00:21:30.380
to worry about both
object level ACL and IAM

00:21:30.380 --> 00:21:33.050
permissions granting
access, and accidentally

00:21:33.050 --> 00:21:35.940
over exposing data that way.

00:21:35.940 --> 00:21:37.730
Additionally, with
bucket policy only, you

00:21:37.730 --> 00:21:40.880
can use this in conjunction
with another GCP feature called

00:21:40.880 --> 00:21:42.290
domain restricted sharing.

00:21:42.290 --> 00:21:44.930
And that'll help you achieve
the use case of keeping

00:21:44.930 --> 00:21:48.780
your data within your domain.

00:21:48.780 --> 00:21:51.320
Now we've seen that over--
since we've launched a beta,

00:21:51.320 --> 00:21:53.810
over a million
buckets have already

00:21:53.810 --> 00:21:55.370
enabled bucket policy only.

00:21:55.370 --> 00:21:59.550
And we hope that you give
this feature a try as well.

00:21:59.550 --> 00:22:03.290
Next we'd like to announce two
new features that really help

00:22:03.290 --> 00:22:05.720
our customers that are
focused on interoperability

00:22:05.720 --> 00:22:07.830
and multi cloud use cases.

00:22:07.830 --> 00:22:10.340
The first of this feature
is service account HMAC,

00:22:10.340 --> 00:22:12.630
which will be in beta shortly.

00:22:12.630 --> 00:22:14.180
Now the problem
that we're trying

00:22:14.180 --> 00:22:16.190
to help solve with
service account HMAC,

00:22:16.190 --> 00:22:19.370
is that tying HMAC credentials
to service accounts

00:22:19.370 --> 00:22:22.243
is a lot better than tying HMAC
credentials to user accounts.

00:22:22.243 --> 00:22:23.660
And if you step
back for a moment,

00:22:23.660 --> 00:22:25.250
and imagine a use case--

00:22:25.250 --> 00:22:27.290
or a scenario rather--

00:22:27.290 --> 00:22:30.530
where you've tied HMAC
credentials to user accounts,

00:22:30.530 --> 00:22:33.530
it may become apparent that when
something happens to that user

00:22:33.530 --> 00:22:36.320
account-- like, the employee
that is tied to that user

00:22:36.320 --> 00:22:38.630
account leaves, or the user
account becomes disabled,

00:22:38.630 --> 00:22:39.840
for example--

00:22:39.840 --> 00:22:43.310
you may risk an outage or
you may risk loss to the data

00:22:43.310 --> 00:22:45.430
that those credentials provided.

00:22:45.430 --> 00:22:48.050
With service account
HMAC, you can prevent this

00:22:48.050 --> 00:22:51.330
from happening by instead
tying your HMAC credentials

00:22:51.330 --> 00:22:53.090
to service accounts.

00:22:53.090 --> 00:22:54.920
Now with this feature,
also offers you

00:22:54.920 --> 00:23:01.490
the ability to completely manage
the lifecycle of HMAC keys.

00:23:01.490 --> 00:23:04.550
Next, we'd like to announce
that V4 signature support is now

00:23:04.550 --> 00:23:05.820
in beta as well.

00:23:05.820 --> 00:23:08.930
So again, this is for our
multi cloud users out there.

00:23:08.930 --> 00:23:13.010
With V4 signature support, you
can make very minimal changes

00:23:13.010 --> 00:23:16.700
to your code base, and
access multiple object stores

00:23:16.700 --> 00:23:18.170
with V4 signing.

00:23:18.170 --> 00:23:22.190
We support this for both HMAC
keys and Google RSA keys,

00:23:22.190 --> 00:23:26.360
and we hope you try
out this as well.

00:23:26.360 --> 00:23:28.760
Now, the features
that I just announced,

00:23:28.760 --> 00:23:31.370
and the ones that Subhasish
mentioned earlier on,

00:23:31.370 --> 00:23:33.500
are just a few of the
features that demonstrate

00:23:33.500 --> 00:23:35.460
our commitment to security.

00:23:35.460 --> 00:23:38.600
I'm also going to go ahead and
demo some of these features,

00:23:38.600 --> 00:23:41.330
and show you how easy it is to
actually use these and put them

00:23:41.330 --> 00:23:42.750
in action.

00:23:42.750 --> 00:23:46.010
So for this demo, let's imagine
that I'm a financial security

00:23:46.010 --> 00:23:50.200
administrator, and I've been
tasked with three things.

00:23:50.200 --> 00:23:52.680
First, I've been tasked
with keeping the data that I

00:23:52.680 --> 00:23:54.840
store within GCS encrypted.

00:23:54.840 --> 00:23:57.120
And as Subhasish mentioned,
everything in GCS

00:23:57.120 --> 00:24:01.380
is already encrypted, but if
you wanted to manage encryption,

00:24:01.380 --> 00:24:02.670
you could do so with KMS.

00:24:02.670 --> 00:24:05.220
And we'll go through a quick
demo that shows you how you do

00:24:05.220 --> 00:24:07.500
that very easily.

00:24:07.500 --> 00:24:11.160
Next, let's say that as this
financial services security

00:24:11.160 --> 00:24:14.400
administrator, I've been told
that, hey, we need to simplify

00:24:14.400 --> 00:24:15.840
permissions management.

00:24:15.840 --> 00:24:18.450
And instead of using
object level ACLs,

00:24:18.450 --> 00:24:21.000
we'll show you how you
can use bucket level IM

00:24:21.000 --> 00:24:23.670
policies through bucket policy
only to make that happen.

00:24:26.520 --> 00:24:30.120
Lastly, let's say that you
have a retention policy that

00:24:30.120 --> 00:24:33.990
wants you to keep your data
secure, or unmodifiable or

00:24:33.990 --> 00:24:36.520
immutable for x number of years.

00:24:36.520 --> 00:24:40.440
Well, bucket lock will help you
do that for any amount of time

00:24:40.440 --> 00:24:42.845
that you set up the
retention policy.

00:24:42.845 --> 00:24:44.220
And we'll go ahead
and we'll demo

00:24:44.220 --> 00:24:47.910
how easy it is to use bucket
lock as well to do this.

00:24:47.910 --> 00:24:51.950
So let me go ahead and
switch over to the demo here.

00:24:55.570 --> 00:24:58.440
So, as you can see, the first
thing that we're going to demo

00:24:58.440 --> 00:25:01.680
is how you can use KMS,
or encryption keys.

00:25:01.680 --> 00:25:04.860
And upload-- whatever data
you upload to a GCS bucket,

00:25:04.860 --> 00:25:06.240
you can pass that key.

00:25:06.240 --> 00:25:09.720
And then it's very easy to do
this entire end to end process.

00:25:09.720 --> 00:25:10.470
So we'll go ahead.

00:25:10.470 --> 00:25:12.720
We're in the cryptographic
keys panel over here.

00:25:12.720 --> 00:25:14.790
We'll click Create key ring.

00:25:14.790 --> 00:25:17.080
And we'll just
create a key ring.

00:25:17.080 --> 00:25:22.230
Let's just name
it something fun.

00:25:22.230 --> 00:25:23.020
OK.

00:25:23.020 --> 00:25:25.490
And then we'll go ahead--
we'll choose a location.

00:25:25.490 --> 00:25:29.007
Let's say we're
choosing us-central1.

00:25:29.007 --> 00:25:31.340
I'm going to-- well, now that
I've created the key ring,

00:25:31.340 --> 00:25:32.655
it's asking me to create a key.

00:25:32.655 --> 00:25:34.280
So I'll go ahead and
I'll create a key.

00:25:37.230 --> 00:25:39.500
As you can tell, I'm pretty
creative in my key names.

00:25:39.500 --> 00:25:44.020
It's the exact same thing as the
key ring, except with a key1.

00:25:44.020 --> 00:25:45.520
So we'll click Create here.

00:25:45.520 --> 00:25:48.120
And now that we've created
this key, we'll go ahead

00:25:48.120 --> 00:25:51.990
and we'll use it to encrypt
data that we upload into GCS.

00:25:51.990 --> 00:25:55.410
So now I've just switched
over to my browser panel here.

00:25:55.410 --> 00:25:57.330
So this is the
view that you would

00:25:57.330 --> 00:26:01.290
see if you were trying to
manage your buckets in GCS.

00:26:01.290 --> 00:26:02.830
I already have one
existing bucket.

00:26:02.830 --> 00:26:06.260
But let's go ahead
and create a bucket.

00:26:06.260 --> 00:26:07.937
So I'll create a bucket.

00:26:07.937 --> 00:26:08.770
I'll give it a name.

00:26:14.110 --> 00:26:14.610
OK.

00:26:14.610 --> 00:26:17.240
We'll choose a
regional location.

00:26:17.240 --> 00:26:21.230
We'll choose us-central1
as this regional location.

00:26:21.230 --> 00:26:24.790
And if you see the Show
Advanced Settings over here,

00:26:24.790 --> 00:26:28.200
it'll ask you to choose
a retention policy.

00:26:28.200 --> 00:26:31.040
So that's not--
that's the next demo.

00:26:31.040 --> 00:26:33.280
But it'll ask you for
encryption as well.

00:26:33.280 --> 00:26:35.120
So you can go ahead
and you can choose

00:26:35.120 --> 00:26:37.080
customer managed key over here.

00:26:37.080 --> 00:26:40.590
And then you can select the
encryption key that you want.

00:26:40.590 --> 00:26:42.870
And this is the encryption
key that I want--

00:26:42.870 --> 00:26:44.060
0401.

00:26:44.060 --> 00:26:46.200
So I'll go ahead and
I'll grant it access.

00:26:46.200 --> 00:26:49.850
So, now that I've granted this
bucket access to that key,

00:26:49.850 --> 00:26:52.710
I'll click Create.

00:26:52.710 --> 00:26:54.300
And now that I have
this bucket, let's

00:26:54.300 --> 00:26:56.830
go ahead and upload some
data to that bucket.

00:26:56.830 --> 00:26:59.100
So we'll upload the CSV file.

00:27:02.620 --> 00:27:04.720
And, once the CSV
file is uploaded,

00:27:04.720 --> 00:27:06.420
you can see that
for the encryption

00:27:06.420 --> 00:27:08.100
it says customer managed key.

00:27:08.100 --> 00:27:09.790
And that's the key
that we created.

00:27:09.790 --> 00:27:11.280
That's the key that we passed.

00:27:11.280 --> 00:27:13.140
So, with just a few
clicks, it's very easy

00:27:13.140 --> 00:27:15.920
to take your KMS key
and upload data into GCS

00:27:15.920 --> 00:27:17.920
and encrypt it with that.

00:27:17.920 --> 00:27:21.193
So, as the storage administrator
for this financial services

00:27:21.193 --> 00:27:23.610
company, I can rest assured
knowing that, OK, this is very

00:27:23.610 --> 00:27:25.940
easy for me to go ahead and do.

00:27:25.940 --> 00:27:28.050
So the next thing
that I was tasked with

00:27:28.050 --> 00:27:30.210
was I was tasked with
trying to simplify

00:27:30.210 --> 00:27:31.780
permissions management.

00:27:31.780 --> 00:27:33.930
So let's take a step
back and imagine

00:27:33.930 --> 00:27:37.710
that you have thousands
of buckets with millions

00:27:37.710 --> 00:27:39.800
of objects in them, and
you're using object level

00:27:39.800 --> 00:27:41.817
ACLs to grant access.

00:27:41.817 --> 00:27:43.650
So we'll click this
bucket that I've already

00:27:43.650 --> 00:27:47.238
set up before the demo, called
bucket with lots of ACLs.

00:27:47.238 --> 00:27:48.780
And when you open
this bucket, you'll

00:27:48.780 --> 00:27:50.800
see that it has a
bunch of objects.

00:27:50.800 --> 00:27:53.130
Now, if this was a
real enterprise bucket,

00:27:53.130 --> 00:27:55.740
I'm sure it would have a
lot more objects than I have

00:27:55.740 --> 00:27:57.090
for this demo-- like millions.

00:27:59.610 --> 00:28:01.240
So there are these
bunch of objects.

00:28:01.240 --> 00:28:03.240
And if we go ahead and
we click Edit Permissions

00:28:03.240 --> 00:28:05.880
on these objects, you can see
that each of these objects

00:28:05.880 --> 00:28:08.610
have their own set of
permissions, or ACLs, tied

00:28:08.610 --> 00:28:09.760
to them.

00:28:09.760 --> 00:28:11.610
So let's say that
for this use case,

00:28:11.610 --> 00:28:14.580
I wanted to make sure that
every object in this bucket,

00:28:14.580 --> 00:28:18.780
I always had owner access to and
my colleague, Subhasish, always

00:28:18.780 --> 00:28:20.700
had reader access to.

00:28:20.700 --> 00:28:23.760
Well, one thing I could do, is
I could go through and confirm

00:28:23.760 --> 00:28:25.410
that this is true, one by one.

00:28:25.410 --> 00:28:26.910
So I can click on
the second object,

00:28:26.910 --> 00:28:28.687
and it seems like,
yep, this is true.

00:28:28.687 --> 00:28:29.520
I have owner access.

00:28:29.520 --> 00:28:30.630
He has reader access.

00:28:30.630 --> 00:28:31.750
Looks good.

00:28:31.750 --> 00:28:33.580
I can click on the third one.

00:28:33.580 --> 00:28:36.450
And I could sit here and
manually go through these one

00:28:36.450 --> 00:28:37.230
by one.

00:28:37.230 --> 00:28:40.672
And then, if I ever discover--
like here, for example--

00:28:40.672 --> 00:28:42.630
that I've given him the
incorrect permissions--

00:28:42.630 --> 00:28:45.480
like he has owner permissions,
but I wanted him to have reader

00:28:45.480 --> 00:28:46.500
permissions--

00:28:46.500 --> 00:28:48.150
I could-- one thing
I could do is I

00:28:48.150 --> 00:28:49.710
could go ahead and change this.

00:28:49.710 --> 00:28:51.900
But, if I was to change
that, it might take me

00:28:51.900 --> 00:28:54.810
a really long time to go
through other objects as well,

00:28:54.810 --> 00:28:56.590
and change it one by one.

00:28:56.590 --> 00:28:58.380
So I don't want this to happen.

00:28:58.380 --> 00:29:01.800
Unfortunately, I don't want to
sit here and go through and do

00:29:01.800 --> 00:29:02.300
that.

00:29:02.300 --> 00:29:04.383
And I want to make sure
that anytime I upload data

00:29:04.383 --> 00:29:06.570
into the bucket,
Subhasish always

00:29:06.570 --> 00:29:08.730
has reader or viewer access.

00:29:08.730 --> 00:29:10.780
Well, bucket policy
only helps you do that.

00:29:10.780 --> 00:29:12.983
It helps you set a
bucket level IM policy.

00:29:12.983 --> 00:29:14.400
That means that
you no longer have

00:29:14.400 --> 00:29:16.720
to manage object ACLs at scale.

00:29:16.720 --> 00:29:19.507
So let's navigate over here
to the Permissions tab.

00:29:19.507 --> 00:29:21.840
And, now that we're in this
Permissions tab, what you'll

00:29:21.840 --> 00:29:24.180
see is that it says you
can simplify access control

00:29:24.180 --> 00:29:25.480
with bucket policy only.

00:29:25.480 --> 00:29:25.980
Great.

00:29:25.980 --> 00:29:27.840
This is exactly
what we want to do.

00:29:27.840 --> 00:29:29.120
We'll click Enable.

00:29:29.120 --> 00:29:31.620
It'll give you
some insight here,

00:29:31.620 --> 00:29:35.010
that you won't be using ACLs
anymore, which is awesome.

00:29:35.010 --> 00:29:36.600
I'll click Enable.

00:29:36.600 --> 00:29:39.640
And, now that I've enabled this,
let's go back into objects.

00:29:39.640 --> 00:29:43.590
And, as you can see, I can no
longer edit the ACL permissions

00:29:43.590 --> 00:29:45.670
on these objects one by one.

00:29:45.670 --> 00:29:48.420
So, if I go back to
Permissions and I

00:29:48.420 --> 00:29:51.330
want to give Subhasish viewer
access to these objects,

00:29:51.330 --> 00:29:52.780
I can do so really easily.

00:29:52.780 --> 00:29:55.470
I can click here on Add Members.

00:29:55.470 --> 00:29:57.420
And then I can
add him over here.

00:30:00.960 --> 00:30:04.110
And I can select the appropriate
role that I want to grant him--

00:30:04.110 --> 00:30:06.480
in this case, storage
object viewer.

00:30:06.480 --> 00:30:08.560
And click Save.

00:30:08.560 --> 00:30:11.230
So, now that I've done
that, if you take a look

00:30:11.230 --> 00:30:13.420
at these objects over
here, essentially,

00:30:13.420 --> 00:30:16.420
what I've basically set up
is the ability for Subhasish

00:30:16.420 --> 00:30:19.810
to have viewer access
to these objects.

00:30:19.810 --> 00:30:22.930
Access for him is not
granted through ACLs anymore.

00:30:22.930 --> 00:30:24.700
And I can rest
assured, making sure

00:30:24.700 --> 00:30:27.800
that any time some data is
uploaded to this bucket,

00:30:27.800 --> 00:30:29.508
Subhasish will always
have viewer access,

00:30:29.508 --> 00:30:31.592
and he'll have the appropriate
permissions I want.

00:30:31.592 --> 00:30:33.190
Now, this is really
useful, again,

00:30:33.190 --> 00:30:36.160
if you have buckets with lots
and lots and lots of objects,

00:30:36.160 --> 00:30:37.690
and you have lots
of users, and you

00:30:37.690 --> 00:30:40.960
don't want to go
through the object ACL

00:30:40.960 --> 00:30:42.940
granularity overhead.

00:30:42.940 --> 00:30:45.130
It's also worth
mentioning that you

00:30:45.130 --> 00:30:46.750
don't have to take
an existing bucket,

00:30:46.750 --> 00:30:48.400
and then turn on
bucket policy only.

00:30:48.400 --> 00:30:50.860
You can do this from the start
by clicking Create bucket.

00:30:50.860 --> 00:30:53.090
And here in the
access control model,

00:30:53.090 --> 00:30:55.540
you'll see that you can
either set permissions

00:30:55.540 --> 00:30:57.970
uniformly at the bucket
level, through bucket policy

00:30:57.970 --> 00:31:02.440
only, or set object level
and bucket level permissions.

00:31:02.440 --> 00:31:03.250
Great.

00:31:03.250 --> 00:31:04.885
So as the storage
administrator, I

00:31:04.885 --> 00:31:06.260
have accomplished
my second task,

00:31:06.260 --> 00:31:08.740
which is simplifying
permissions management.

00:31:08.740 --> 00:31:10.720
Now, the last thing that
I've been tasked with

00:31:10.720 --> 00:31:14.470
is keeping data that
I upload into GCS

00:31:14.470 --> 00:31:18.190
unmodifiable or immutable
for x number of years.

00:31:18.190 --> 00:31:20.440
So you can imagine that
you've been handed a retention

00:31:20.440 --> 00:31:23.620
policy that says, hey, upload
data into this GCS bucket,

00:31:23.620 --> 00:31:26.410
but keep it immutable
for 10 years.

00:31:26.410 --> 00:31:28.570
GCS makes this really,
really easy to do,

00:31:28.570 --> 00:31:29.823
and we'll show you how.

00:31:29.823 --> 00:31:31.240
So we'll click
Create bucket here.

00:31:34.050 --> 00:31:36.840
And I'll just name it that.

00:31:36.840 --> 00:31:39.840
I'll choose the regional
storage location

00:31:39.840 --> 00:31:42.520
that I've always been
choosing-- us-central1.

00:31:42.520 --> 00:31:44.370
And if I go into Show
Advanced Settings,

00:31:44.370 --> 00:31:46.110
you'll see that
there's a retention

00:31:46.110 --> 00:31:48.360
policy over here, which is
exactly what we want to do.

00:31:48.360 --> 00:31:50.290
We want to set a
retention policy.

00:31:50.290 --> 00:31:53.220
So I can set this for 10 years,
which means that every time I

00:31:53.220 --> 00:31:55.020
upload data into
this bucket, I won't

00:31:55.020 --> 00:31:58.630
be able to change anything
about it for 10 years.

00:31:58.630 --> 00:32:01.248
I won't be able to modify it,
won't be able to delete it.

00:32:01.248 --> 00:32:02.790
But because I'm sure
none of you want

00:32:02.790 --> 00:32:06.450
to stay here until 2029 to
prove that this feature works,

00:32:06.450 --> 00:32:07.710
I will instead--

00:32:07.710 --> 00:32:09.360
for purposes of this demo--

00:32:09.360 --> 00:32:10.990
change this to 30 seconds.

00:32:10.990 --> 00:32:13.150
So we're going from 10
years to 30 seconds here,

00:32:13.150 --> 00:32:15.630
but I think you guys
will get the point.

00:32:15.630 --> 00:32:19.140
So essentially, if I click
Create on this bucket,

00:32:19.140 --> 00:32:22.140
what I've done is, I've created
a bucket where every time I

00:32:22.140 --> 00:32:26.960
upload some data into
it-- like this CSV file--

00:32:26.960 --> 00:32:30.660
after upload, I won't be able to
overwrite or delete that file.

00:32:30.660 --> 00:32:33.450
So if I click Delete
here, it'll say,

00:32:33.450 --> 00:32:35.570
hey, you can't delete
this object yet.

00:32:35.570 --> 00:32:39.650
You won't be able to do it until
this retention policy is over--

00:32:39.650 --> 00:32:42.618
until the 30 seconds
have passed by.

00:32:42.618 --> 00:32:44.785
So you can imagine that for
a storage administrator,

00:32:44.785 --> 00:32:46.332
this is very easy to set up.

00:32:46.332 --> 00:32:48.290
If someone says, hey, we
need to hold this data

00:32:48.290 --> 00:32:51.650
for 10 years, 15 years, whatever
your security policy says,

00:32:51.650 --> 00:32:55.370
or whatever regulation
you're saying says--

00:32:55.370 --> 00:32:58.170
you just, with a few clicks,
you can make that happen.

00:32:58.170 --> 00:33:00.810
And after those--
after that time is up--

00:33:00.810 --> 00:33:03.080
so in this scenario, after
the 30 seconds are up

00:33:03.080 --> 00:33:04.820
or the 10 years are
up-- you can go ahead

00:33:04.820 --> 00:33:07.880
and do whatever you'd like
to do with the objects.

00:33:07.880 --> 00:33:13.330
So I'm assuming 30 seconds are
up, so we'll click Delete here.

00:33:13.330 --> 00:33:16.930
And as you can see, after that
retention time period is over,

00:33:16.930 --> 00:33:20.500
it's very easy to go ahead
and modify that object.

00:33:20.500 --> 00:33:26.110
So in conclusion, the
things that we just demoed--

00:33:26.110 --> 00:33:30.070
bucket policy only, KMS,
and also bucket lock--

00:33:30.070 --> 00:33:31.840
are just three of
the many security

00:33:31.840 --> 00:33:33.610
features that GCS provides.

00:33:33.610 --> 00:33:37.780
Everything is meant to be very
intuitive and easy to use.

00:33:37.780 --> 00:33:40.120
And we hope that this
gives you a sense

00:33:40.120 --> 00:33:42.400
of the level of commitment
that we have into keeping

00:33:42.400 --> 00:33:44.920
your data secure on GCS.

00:33:44.920 --> 00:33:47.800
With that, I'd like
to turn it over

00:33:47.800 --> 00:33:51.830
to our friends from
Twitter, Chad and Chris,

00:33:51.830 --> 00:33:56.912
who will talk about how GCS is
being used at Twitter securely.

00:33:56.912 --> 00:33:58.350
And you can switch over to the--

00:33:58.350 --> 00:33:58.850
right.

00:33:58.850 --> 00:34:00.142
CHAD HODGES: Thank you, Risham.

00:34:00.142 --> 00:34:02.850
[APPLAUSE]

00:34:06.090 --> 00:34:06.590
Great.

00:34:06.590 --> 00:34:09.766
Thank you very much
for the warm welcome.

00:34:09.766 --> 00:34:16.650
Let me get to our slides.

00:34:16.650 --> 00:34:18.070
Wonderful.

00:34:18.070 --> 00:34:20.741
All that preparation to
say hi, I'm Chad Hodges.

00:34:20.741 --> 00:34:22.449
CHRISTOPHER ULHERR:
And I'm Chris Ulherr,

00:34:22.449 --> 00:34:26.002
and we're from the public
cloud services team at Twitter.

00:34:26.002 --> 00:34:27.460
And we're here
today to talk to you

00:34:27.460 --> 00:34:30.130
about how Twitter
uses Google Cloud,

00:34:30.130 --> 00:34:32.380
and specifically, how we're
using some of the features

00:34:32.380 --> 00:34:34.360
talked about earlier
in the slides

00:34:34.360 --> 00:34:39.550
to help improve our security and
meet our security objectives.

00:34:39.550 --> 00:34:41.409
CHAD HODGES: So just
to start off here.

00:34:41.409 --> 00:34:43.300
Hopefully, some of
you are tweeting this.

00:34:43.300 --> 00:34:45.508
But Twitter's purpose, and
how we position ourselves,

00:34:45.508 --> 00:34:48.159
is that we're here to serve
the public conversation.

00:34:48.159 --> 00:34:51.280
And we want people to stay
informed, inform others,

00:34:51.280 --> 00:34:52.989
and talk about what
matters in a way that

00:34:52.989 --> 00:34:55.600
helps society progress.

00:34:55.600 --> 00:34:57.980
For our part, being part of
the platform organization,

00:34:57.980 --> 00:34:58.900
there are some
quick facts I want

00:34:58.900 --> 00:35:01.067
to share with you about
Twitter, if you didn't know.

00:35:01.067 --> 00:35:03.640
321 million monthly
active users,

00:35:03.640 --> 00:35:07.930
a peak of 143,199
tweets per second.

00:35:07.930 --> 00:35:09.372
We have a significant
on premises

00:35:09.372 --> 00:35:11.080
footprint, which is
maybe a strange thing

00:35:11.080 --> 00:35:12.340
to say at a cloud conference.

00:35:12.340 --> 00:35:15.167
But Twitter has hundreds of
thousands of physical machines.

00:35:15.167 --> 00:35:16.750
When you talk about
the companies that

00:35:16.750 --> 00:35:19.390
are doing what we're
doing at our scale,

00:35:19.390 --> 00:35:21.580
it's a handful, at most.

00:35:21.580 --> 00:35:24.835
But at the same time, we're
migrating targeted workloads

00:35:24.835 --> 00:35:25.720
to the Google Cloud.

00:35:25.720 --> 00:35:28.120
That's the partly cloudy
t-shirt I'm actually wearing.

00:35:28.120 --> 00:35:30.640
Hopefully, some of you got to
take advantage of the panel

00:35:30.640 --> 00:35:31.990
yesterday about that.

00:35:31.990 --> 00:35:35.230
And when I say targeted,
that might imply small,

00:35:35.230 --> 00:35:38.110
but we're actually
moving 300 petabytes

00:35:38.110 --> 00:35:42.430
worth of Hadoop data to GCS.

00:35:42.430 --> 00:35:45.140
And that session was
yesterday, as I mentioned.

00:35:45.140 --> 00:35:48.790
And we're clearly
going to be hybrid.

00:35:48.790 --> 00:35:50.590
We spent a lot of
time, a lot of energy,

00:35:50.590 --> 00:35:52.223
analyzing that decision.

00:35:52.223 --> 00:35:53.640
And, if you're
interested in that,

00:35:53.640 --> 00:35:55.390
it's not something
for this session,

00:35:55.390 --> 00:35:58.450
but there's a great video
of Derek Lyon from Google

00:35:58.450 --> 00:35:59.512
Next in London last year.

00:35:59.512 --> 00:36:00.470
You can check that out.

00:36:00.470 --> 00:36:02.625
And I would encourage you
to do so because a lot

00:36:02.625 --> 00:36:04.750
of the methodologies we
used-- a lot of things that

00:36:04.750 --> 00:36:06.580
informed our decision-- are
things you should be looking

00:36:06.580 --> 00:36:07.080
at as well.

00:36:09.620 --> 00:36:13.310
So, for our team, which is the
public cloud services team,

00:36:13.310 --> 00:36:18.160
we have some high level
pieces to our mission.

00:36:18.160 --> 00:36:20.873
And against the backdrop
of that massive migration,

00:36:20.873 --> 00:36:23.290
and the recognition that there
are other teams in addition

00:36:23.290 --> 00:36:25.373
to Hadoop who can get value
from the Google Cloud,

00:36:25.373 --> 00:36:29.267
how do we engage with the
other teams at Twitter?

00:36:29.267 --> 00:36:30.850
And it's really
important to recognize

00:36:30.850 --> 00:36:32.790
that we have to scale.

00:36:32.790 --> 00:36:34.600
We can't be gatekeepers.

00:36:34.600 --> 00:36:36.460
There are a lot
of smart, capable,

00:36:36.460 --> 00:36:38.410
and inventive people
at Twitter that

00:36:38.410 --> 00:36:41.320
can come up with amazing ways to
use the cloud to take advantage

00:36:41.320 --> 00:36:42.640
of Google Cloud.

00:36:42.640 --> 00:36:45.260
And we have to make
sure that that happens.

00:36:45.260 --> 00:36:48.370
And that it's done with as
little friction as possible.

00:36:48.370 --> 00:36:50.020
And then, second,
because of our scale

00:36:50.020 --> 00:36:51.700
and because of our
history, we sometimes

00:36:51.700 --> 00:36:53.800
need support or change
from Google in order

00:36:53.800 --> 00:36:54.800
to use their offerings.

00:36:54.800 --> 00:36:58.058
And, to be blunt, I'm delighted
with the offerings that

00:36:58.058 --> 00:36:59.350
have been discussed here today.

00:36:59.350 --> 00:37:01.157
They've made our
job's a lot easier.

00:37:01.157 --> 00:37:02.740
They've made me a
lot more comfortable

00:37:02.740 --> 00:37:03.877
with what we're doing.

00:37:03.877 --> 00:37:06.460
And ultimately our goals are to
make sure that our customers--

00:37:06.460 --> 00:37:09.113
that the internal developers
and users at Twitter--

00:37:09.113 --> 00:37:10.780
can leverage what
Google offers in a way

00:37:10.780 --> 00:37:14.560
that's secure, reliable,
supportable, and repeatable.

00:37:14.560 --> 00:37:16.678
And again, we need
to be able to do that

00:37:16.678 --> 00:37:18.970
without falling into the trap
of toil, for those of you

00:37:18.970 --> 00:37:22.460
with the SRE background.

00:37:22.460 --> 00:37:25.040
And part of this is
that we're weighing

00:37:25.040 --> 00:37:26.630
convenience versus security.

00:37:26.630 --> 00:37:30.050
So Twitter is a public
platform as you're all aware.

00:37:30.050 --> 00:37:31.580
Twitter data is unique, right?

00:37:31.580 --> 00:37:33.920
It's a public conversation.

00:37:33.920 --> 00:37:36.100
We make pieces of that
available via APIs,

00:37:36.100 --> 00:37:38.270
but we also have to
make sure that we're

00:37:38.270 --> 00:37:41.240
taking into account privacy
and data protection,

00:37:41.240 --> 00:37:45.320
and we're adhering to the trust
that our users place in us.

00:37:45.320 --> 00:37:47.240
If developers
violate our policies,

00:37:47.240 --> 00:37:49.020
we're going to take
appropriate action.

00:37:49.020 --> 00:37:52.220
We also, in our role,
have to make sure

00:37:52.220 --> 00:37:54.740
that we have access
controls and compliance,

00:37:54.740 --> 00:37:57.380
and things don't
happen on accident.

00:37:57.380 --> 00:38:00.620
And to do that, we're leveraging
VPC Service Controls, domain

00:38:00.620 --> 00:38:03.440
restricted sharing,
and bucket policies--

00:38:03.440 --> 00:38:05.600
pardon me-- bucket policy only.

00:38:05.600 --> 00:38:07.910
And there are pieces
of our security policy.

00:38:07.910 --> 00:38:10.620
Obviously, there are
other things that we do.

00:38:10.620 --> 00:38:12.560
But this is greatly
simplified the approach.

00:38:12.560 --> 00:38:14.810
It's made it a lot easier
to be comfortable that we're

00:38:14.810 --> 00:38:16.470
doing the right thing.

00:38:16.470 --> 00:38:20.510
And it's also made it so
that we're secure by default.

00:38:20.510 --> 00:38:22.880
And a large part of the
Hadoop migration project

00:38:22.880 --> 00:38:25.310
is making sure that
we've done that.

00:38:25.310 --> 00:38:27.050
And we've developed
several services--

00:38:27.050 --> 00:38:28.810
we touched on those yesterday--

00:38:28.810 --> 00:38:31.560
the demigod services--
in that panel.

00:38:31.560 --> 00:38:33.750
And as we move other
workloads to the cloud,

00:38:33.750 --> 00:38:35.870
we'll continue to
architect our environment

00:38:35.870 --> 00:38:39.148
to make it easy to
do the right thing.

00:38:39.148 --> 00:38:40.190
CHRISTOPHER ULHERR: Yeah.

00:38:40.190 --> 00:38:43.010
As Chad mentioned, we're
hyper focused on security,

00:38:43.010 --> 00:38:46.250
and regardless of intent,
a misconfigured bucket

00:38:46.250 --> 00:38:48.550
is one of the easiest
ways to expose data.

00:38:48.550 --> 00:38:50.510
And it's something we
audit for, and something

00:38:50.510 --> 00:38:52.040
that we strive to prevent.

00:38:52.040 --> 00:38:53.660
And a key piece
of achieving that

00:38:53.660 --> 00:38:55.760
is using the tools
that Google provides.

00:38:55.760 --> 00:38:58.760
So we've leveraged both
the VPC Service Controls,

00:38:58.760 --> 00:39:01.010
and the domain
restricted sharing,

00:39:01.010 --> 00:39:03.140
and provided feedback
to Google in the process

00:39:03.140 --> 00:39:05.220
to help achieve that.

00:39:05.220 --> 00:39:06.860
And so with the VPC's
service controls,

00:39:06.860 --> 00:39:08.720
it helped us
establish a perimeter

00:39:08.720 --> 00:39:12.170
so that we knew who exactly
can access the data that's

00:39:12.170 --> 00:39:13.220
in the storage buckets.

00:39:13.220 --> 00:39:15.500
And then domain
restricted sharing--

00:39:15.500 --> 00:39:18.483
we can say, for example, if
I have a BigQuery data set,

00:39:18.483 --> 00:39:20.900
and somebody is going to attempt
to share that, they don't

00:39:20.900 --> 00:39:22.530
share it outside of our domain.

00:39:22.530 --> 00:39:24.350
So it helps put that
control on top of it.

00:39:24.350 --> 00:39:27.290
And those help increase
our security posture.

00:39:30.225 --> 00:39:31.600
CHAD HODGES: And
with that, we'll

00:39:31.600 --> 00:39:33.019
hand it back to Subhasish.

00:39:39.978 --> 00:39:42.020
SUBHASISH CHAKRABORTY:
Thank you, Chad and Chris.

00:39:42.020 --> 00:39:44.425
[APPLAUSE]

00:39:47.320 --> 00:39:49.660
I think this was
very instructive,

00:39:49.660 --> 00:39:53.260
to understand how a company
like Twitter, with so much PII,

00:39:53.260 --> 00:39:56.890
and so much really
important information

00:39:56.890 --> 00:39:59.380
uses GCS, and, at the
same time, ensures

00:39:59.380 --> 00:40:02.120
that their data is secure.

00:40:02.120 --> 00:40:06.550
So in conclusion, I would like
to touch upon a few themes

00:40:06.550 --> 00:40:08.810
that we have explored
in this session.

00:40:08.810 --> 00:40:10.600
First of all,
Google Cloud Storage

00:40:10.600 --> 00:40:15.160
is the unified blob storage,
or object storage platform

00:40:15.160 --> 00:40:19.030
in which you can store
your data to unlock

00:40:19.030 --> 00:40:25.780
the entire power of Google
Cloud as a data platform.

00:40:25.780 --> 00:40:28.810
We have planet
scale scalability,

00:40:28.810 --> 00:40:31.990
but at the same time, it's
a very consistent, unified

00:40:31.990 --> 00:40:34.840
and simple system.

00:40:34.840 --> 00:40:38.620
Our customers use GCS for a
range of different use cases.

00:40:38.620 --> 00:40:42.190
When you are using GCS,
one of the biggest benefits

00:40:42.190 --> 00:40:44.470
that you get is there is
a lot of security, which

00:40:44.470 --> 00:40:46.570
is built in by
design and default.

00:40:46.570 --> 00:40:50.980
So you do not have to think of
building every single security

00:40:50.980 --> 00:40:51.970
feature from scratch.

00:40:51.970 --> 00:40:54.700
There is a lot that we
already take care of, right?

00:40:54.700 --> 00:40:59.390
And then in addition to having
very good default controls,

00:40:59.390 --> 00:41:02.110
we also expose a lot
of custom controls,

00:41:02.110 --> 00:41:05.270
based on your use case
where it makes sense.

00:41:05.270 --> 00:41:07.350
And in this session,
as you have seen,

00:41:07.350 --> 00:41:09.100
we have talked about
some features we have

00:41:09.100 --> 00:41:10.510
launched over the last year.

00:41:10.510 --> 00:41:14.010
And then you also saw some fresh
features today and some demos.

00:41:14.010 --> 00:41:15.790
You would see this
continuing theme

00:41:15.790 --> 00:41:18.970
that our main focus
from the product team

00:41:18.970 --> 00:41:20.200
is to listen to you--

00:41:20.200 --> 00:41:22.690
our most important
constituency, the customers--

00:41:22.690 --> 00:41:25.370
and to innovate where it
really matters for you.

00:41:25.370 --> 00:41:27.270
So this is no surprise
then that there

00:41:27.270 --> 00:41:29.650
is a lot of large
customers and enterprises

00:41:29.650 --> 00:41:34.600
who use GCS to do a lot of
amazing things with their data,

00:41:34.600 --> 00:41:38.050
at the same time, ensure that
the data is fully secure.

00:41:38.050 --> 00:41:40.930
So with that, I hope
you guys continue

00:41:40.930 --> 00:41:44.080
to have an amazing NEXT.

00:41:44.080 --> 00:41:48.130
And for those of you who
haven't tried GCS yet,

00:41:48.130 --> 00:41:50.640
this would be a good
time to give GCS a try.

00:41:50.640 --> 00:41:51.140
Thank you.

00:41:51.140 --> 00:41:52.670
Thank you everyone for
attending this session.

00:41:52.670 --> 00:41:53.570
Really appreciate.

00:41:53.570 --> 00:41:55.070
[APPLAUSE]

00:41:55.070 --> 00:41:58.420
